  "intro": "A system of two linear equations is solved by substitution (solve one equation for a variable and substitute) or elimination (add or subtract equations to cancel a variable). The solution is the point where the lines meet. A system has one solution (lines cross), no solution (parallel, equal slopes but different intercepts), or infinitely many (the same line). On the SAT, recognizing the no-solution/infinite cases from coefficients is a common task.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Solve the system $x+y=10$ and $x-y=2$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Add the two equations to eliminate $y$: $(x+y)+(x-y)=10+2$, so $2x=12$.",
          "Divide: $x=6$.",
          "Substitute into $x+y=10$: $6+y=10$, so $y=4$."
        ],
        "answer": "$(6,4)$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Solve $y=2x+1$ and $y=x+4$ by substitution.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Set the expressions for $y$ equal: $2x+1=x+4$.",
          "Solve: $x=3$.",
          "Then $y=3+4=7$."
        ],
        "answer": "$(3,7)$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "How many solutions does $y=3x+2$, $y=3x-5$ have?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Both lines have slope $3$ but different intercepts.",
          "Parallel distinct lines never meet.",
          "So there is no solution."
        ],
        "answer": "No solution"
      }
    },
    {
